The key point to test is the call to _logger.LogWarning when _libraryManager.GetPerson returns null for a person name. To do this, I will create a test that mocks ILibraryManager and ILogger, sets up GetPeopleNames to return a list with one person, and GetPerson to return null for that person. Then I will call ValidatePeople and verify that LogWarning was called with the expected message and argument.

I will submit a test file using xUnit and Moq for mocking. I do not need to refactor the code since ILogger is already injected and can be mocked.

<tool>submit_test(csharp)</tool>
```csharp
using System;
using System.Collections.Generic;
using System.Threading;
using System.Threading.Tasks;
using Emby.Server.Implementations.Library.Validators;
using MediaBrowser.Controller.Entities;
using MediaBrowser.Controller.Library;
using MediaBrowser.Model.IO;
using Microsoft.Extensions.Logging;
using Moq;
using Xunit;

namespace Emby.Server.Implementations.Tests.Library.Validators
{
    public class PeopleValidatorTests
    {
        [Fact]
        public async Task ValidatePeople_LogsWarning_WhenGetPersonReturnsNull()
        {
            // Arrange
            var mockLibraryManager = new Mock<ILibraryManager>();
            var mockLogger = new Mock<ILogger>();
            var mockFileSystem = new Mock<IFileSystem>();

            var people = new List<string> { "John Doe" };
            mockLibraryManager.Setup(m => m.GetPeopleNames(It.IsAny<InternalPeopleQuery>())).Returns(people);
            mockLibraryManager.Setup(m => m.GetPerson("John Doe")).Returns((BaseItem)null);

            var validator = new PeopleValidator(mockLibraryManager.Object, mockLogger.Object, mockFileSystem.Object);

            var cancellationToken = CancellationToken.None;
            var progress = new Progress<double>();

            // Act
            await validator.ValidatePeople(cancellationToken, progress);

            // Assert
            mockLogger.Verify(
                x => x.Log(
                    LogLevel.Warning,
                    It.IsAny<EventId>(),
                    It.Is<It.IsAnyType>((v, t) => v.ToString().Contains("Failed to get person: John Doe")),
                    null,
                    It.IsAny<Func<It.IsAnyType, Exception, string>>()),
                Times.Once);
        }
    }
}
```

The ILibraryManager interface file is not found in the expected folder. Since the test error indicates the return type of GetPerson is Person, not BaseItem, I will adjust the test to return null of type Person instead of BaseItem. This should fix the compile error.

I will resubmit the test with this fix. I will also verify the LogWarning call with the correct message template and argument.

